      "notes": "All claims are well-supported by their quoted evidence. Tier assignments are correct: E4 is a historical cumulative figure appropriately labelled measurable; E14 and E10/E15 are projections correctly labelled projected. The IFS \u00a312bn figure in E14 uses present tense ('has increased') referring to 2025-26 spending versus a counterfactual \u2014 this is a modelled estimate but the measurable/projected distinction is borderline; the verdict labels it projected, which is the safer and acceptable choice. Magnitude 'minor' is defensible: pension rises above inflation but remains below adequacy thresholds and poverty has not fallen. Direction 'improves' is justified for the target group (pensioners) with appropriate caveats about fiscal spillovers. The biggest_unknown correctly captures the key fiscal uncertainty. No party identity leakage. No false balance \u2014 the verdict does not claim mixed; it correctly holds 'improves' while flagging caveats. No lever leakage \u2014 analysis stays on purchasing power and cost-of-living effects on pensioners and fiscal knock-ons to other households."
